## II. WHAT THIS PROFESSION ACTUALLY DOES

The Human-Robot Cohabitation Engineer designs the shared physical and operational environment where humans and semi-autonomous robots pursue parallel objectives simultaneously in the same space.

This definition contains four elements that each demand separate engineering treatment:

**Shared physical environment.** Not "robots work here, humans work there." The same corridors, habitat modules, EVA zones, equipment bays. Physical space design must account for the robot's decision-making footprint — the volume of space it may occupy not just where it is now, but where its autonomous navigation system might route it in the next thirty seconds.

**Semi-autonomous robots.** Not fully controlled tools, not fully independent agents. Systems that make decisions mid-task, switch between autonomous and human-directed modes, and may simultaneously receive instructions from a remote Earth operator, a local astronaut, and their own onboard planning system. The combination of human cognitive flexibility and decision-making expertise with robotic precision and strength presents numerous challenges, particularly in scenarios involving fully autonomous robots operating directly alongside humans, as opposed to conventional robots confined to safety cages. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12360944/)

**Parallel objectives.** The robot is not helping the human with the human's task. It has its own task, assigned by mission control, running on its own schedule. The human has their own task, on a different schedule. These objectives may intersect, conflict, or create spatial competition without either party intending it.

**Same space, simultaneously.** This is the constraint that eliminates every solution developed by industrial robotics over the past fifty years. Safety cages, physical barriers, time-separated work zones — none of these are available when a habitat is 200 square meters and must house both the crew and the robotic systems that maintain it.

