## III. THE COGNITIVE STRESS PROBLEM: WHY THIS IS DIFFERENT FROM TOOL-SHARING

The central premise of this profession is that the cognitive stress of sharing space with decision-making machines is qualitatively distinct from sharing space with tools. The research quantifies what that distinction looks like in the human body.

Studies measuring acute stress responses from ECG and EDA data show that on average, participants feel more stress during encounters with autonomous robots compared to baselines, and stress increases during navigation behavior compared to search behavior. Participants also feel more stress encountering multiple robots compared to a single robot. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.17270)

This physiological data carries an immediate design implication. A lunar colony with one autonomous rover is a different stress environment from a colony with three rovers, two construction robots, and a habitat maintenance drone all operating semi-autonomously in the same space. The stress does not scale linearly — it compounds. And it reveals a design trap.

When a robot possesses a high degree of autonomy, individuals feel anxious due to a lack of a sense of control over the robot. In contrast, when a robot exhibits low autonomy, it can enhance individuals' sense of control, thereby reducing feelings of anxiety. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2451958825003380)

Low autonomy feels psychologically safer. High autonomy is operationally necessary. The Cohabitation Engineer must design shared environments that deliver the operational benefits of high autonomy while managing the psychological cost — not by deceiving colonists about what robots are doing, but by designing behavioral signals and physical environments so that high-autonomy operation does not feel like a loss of control.

But this blueprint introduces a second premise that previous frameworks ignored: **humans under stress are not passive recipients of discomfort. They are active problem-solvers who will modify their environment to recover the agency they feel they have lost.** On the Moon, that behavioral reality is not a human resources problem. It is an engineering constraint.

