## V. FAILURE MODE 2 — SENSOR POLLUTION AND THE SHARED LOCALIZATION GRID

Because CIMON was the first free-flyer to operate on the ISS, safety was a significant concern. Even if CIMON's shape meant it would not hurt anyone, colliding with astronauts in an already-cramped space could quickly become annoying. To avoid this, CIMON was equipped with 12 ultrasonic sensors that enable it to detect obstacles and incoming objects. [Connector Supplier](https://connectorsupplier.com/cimon-says-design-lessons-from-a-robot-assistant-in-space/)

CIMON is a single robot with 12 sensors in a large station module. Now consider: a lunar habitat module houses a habitat maintenance drone with ultrasonic sensors, a terrain vehicle parked inside the airlock cycling through LiDAR scans, a medical assistant robot running infrared proximity sensing, and the colony's structural monitoring system pulsing RF signals through the walls. Every one of these systems is independently scanning the same metallic, pressurized volume simultaneously.

The result is an invisible infrastructure catastrophe. Ultrasonic signals from one robot echo off metal walls and register as false obstacles in another robot's sensor array. LiDAR pulses interfere with infrared proximity detection. RF signals from structural monitoring create noise floors that degrade every other wireless system in the module. The robots begin making navigation and proximity decisions based on sensor data that is partially fiction — artifacts of their own collective interference.

And then there is the human cost. Two separately developed astronaut assistance robots successfully communicated and cooperated across two different IT networks for the first time in the ICHIBAN mission. [ibm](https://uk.newsroom.ibm.com/german-japanese-space-agencies-achieve-robotics-milestone-aboard-international-space-station) What ICHIBAN demonstrated in a large station module becomes a sensor ecology crisis when the same principle is applied to ten robotic systems in a compact pressurized habitat. The ultrasonic emissions alone — inaudible individually, accumulative collectively — create a stress-inducing acoustic environment that colonists cannot identify as robotic in origin but will experience as persistent low-level unease.

**The architectural solution has two components: Dampening Profiles and a Shared Localization Grid.**

**Acoustic and Electromagnetic Dampening Profiles** are mandatory specifications for every habitat interior surface in shared human-robot spaces. The interior walls, floors, and ceilings of any module where multiple robotic systems operate are lined with materials selected specifically to absorb — not reflect — ultrasonic, LiDAR, and RF signals. The habitat interior stops being an acoustic and electromagnetic mirror and starts behaving like a controlled sensing environment. Every robotic system's sensor footprint is specified against the dampened environment, not the reflective metal shell.

The Dampening Profile is determined before a single habitat component is manufactured. It cannot be retrofitted after construction without module redesign. This makes it one of the Cohabitation Engineer's earliest deliverables in the colony's design cycle — a specification document that must exist before the materials supply list is finalized.

**The Shared Localization Grid** replaces independent active scanning with a single, colony-wide passive positioning infrastructure. Rather than every robot independently emitting signals to locate itself and detect obstacles, all robots draw from a shared grid of embedded passive beacons throughout the habitat — a fixed, known reference network that provides localization data without any robot needing to emit a single active scan.

Active scanning — the pinging of sensors off walls and obstacles — is reserved for situations where the shared grid is insufficient: novel obstacle detection, close-proximity collision avoidance in the final approach to an object, and emergency navigation when grid coverage fails. In routine operation, the grid handles localization silently. The habitat stops being a reverberant chamber of competing robotic emissions and becomes a quiet, cooperative sensing environment.

The operational benefit is reliability. The psychological benefit is silence. Both matter enormously in a sealed environment where humans live and work for months.

