## VI. FAILURE MODE 3 — HUD FATIGUE AND THE EXCEPTION-ONLY PERIPHERAL INTERFACE

The previous version of this blueprint proposed an Operational Intent Broadcast system — a display showing colonists where every nearby autonomous robot is, what it is doing, and whether any collision with their planned activity is anticipated. The proposal was sound in principle and flawed in execution.

The flaw is alert fatigue. A lunar habitat with a dozen autonomous systems operating around the clock generates hundreds of trajectory recalculations per hour. If every recalculation that involves a human's vicinity triggers a display notification, colonists will be swiping away minor trajectory alerts from a maintenance drone within their first week of operation. They will learn to ignore the system. And when a heavy terrain vehicle changes modes unexpectedly in a shared corridor, the alert that actually matters will be dismissed with the same habituated gesture they have used to clear the previous four hundred irrelevant notifications.

This is not a hypothetical. It is documented in every safety-critical environment that has implemented continuous alerting systems — aviation, nuclear operations, surgical suites. Alert fatigue is not a failure of human attention. It is a predictable human response to an interface design that lacks appropriate signal hierarchy.

**The architectural solution is the Exception-Only Peripheral Interface.**

The system operates on a single rule: it is invisible until it matters.

In baseline operation — robots moving normally, trajectories non-conflicting, modes stable — colonists receive no signal whatsoever from the robotic situational awareness system. No displays, no sounds, no notifications. Zero.

The system activates only when a robot's recalculated trajectory intersects with the human's calculated path-density footprint within a three-minute window. "Path-density footprint" is a composite model of where the colonist is likely to be, derived from their current location, their declared task, their historical movement patterns in this area of the habitat, and their current velocity vector. It is not "where they are standing" — it is "the probabilistic volume of space they are likely to occupy over the next three minutes."

When intersection is predicted, the alert is **haptic and auditory-spatial, never visual text.**

Haptic: a brief, distinctive vibration pattern in the colonist's worksuit or wrist device that communicates direction — the vibration source indicates which direction the robot is approaching from. No interpretation required. No reading required. No glance toward a display required.

Auditory-spatial: a directional low-frequency hum, spatially rendered through the habitat's audio system to appear to originate from the direction of the robot in question. The colonist hears, from their right, a sound that tells them without conscious analysis: something significant is approaching from the right.

Two signals, no visual load, no text, no interaction required. The colonist's response can be entirely physical — step aside, pause, continue — without any break in their cognitive engagement with their primary task.

Visual display is available on demand only. A colonist who wants to see the full robotic situational picture can access it — but the system never pushes it at them. This design principle, **push exception, pull detail**, is the fundamental inversion that separates a psychologically sustainable awareness system from one that trains its users to ignore it.

