## X. THE REGULATORY INFLECTION POINT: ISO 10218:2025

Earth has produced the most relevant safety framework this profession can learn from — and it arrives with an important conceptual shift.

In 2025, the ANSI/A3 R15.06-2024 standard was revised — the first major update in more than a decade. The terms "collaborative robot" and "collaborative operation" will no longer be found in the revised ISO 10218. "Collaborative application" is used instead, as only the actual use of the robot can be designed, tested, and confirmed as a collaborative application. [IDEC US](https://www.idec.com/en-us/blog/iso-10218-updates-revisions-background-part-2)

Safety is not a property of the robot. It is a property of the application — the specific combination of robot, task, environment, and human interaction pattern. On the Moon, this principle extends beyond physical safety to psychological safety. A robot that is mechanically safe but psychologically corrosive is a flawed collaborative application by definition.

Every one of the five failure modes addressed in this blueprint is an application-level failure, not a robot-level failure. The robot that triggers Alert Fatigue is not a bad robot — it is a robot deployed in a bad application design. The robot that generates Space Superstition events is not malfunctioning — it is a correctly functioning robot in an application that has no Clear-State Affirmation protocol. The Cohabitation Engineer's job is to design applications, not robots.

