Zone definition Hard‑coded, unconditional robot‑forbidden zones integrated into physical architecture of habitat
Included areas Crew quarters, personal hygiene modules, designated rest areas
Excluded areas Common corridors, workspaces, EVA airlocks, medical bays
Enforcement mechanism Structural: electromagnetic shielding, signal absorption panels, mechanical barriers. Not software‑overridable
Override condition Life‑threatening emergency only, confirmed by redundant non‑robotic sensors
Zone legibility Absolute boundary in robot navigation architecture—robot cannot assess whether entering is justified; cannot enter
Zone generosity Minimum 20% of pressurized volume reserved as human‑only space (recommended; parametric to mission configuration)
Public declaration Every colonist knows exactly which physical areas are in the Privacy Cloak and exactly what “cloak” means

Design Principles

1. The zones are unconditional. No operational priority overrides the Privacy Cloak except a confirmed life‑threatening emergency. The moment colonists learn the zone can be penetrated, the psychological sanctuary collapses.
2. The zones are legible to robots as absolute boundaries. Not high‑priority avoidance zones—hard navigation stops. Full stop.
3. The zones are publicly declared and spatially generous. Ambiguity destroys the benefit. Genuine sanctuary, not a single sleeping pod.

Validation Research

Research on isolated, confined environments (ICE) has established that confinement and isolation produce measurable psychological stress: fatigue, altered circadian rhythm, neurocognitive impairment, and sleep disturbance. Studies of the Salyut 6 space station have documented interpersonal and individual strain among crews under isolation. Antarctic winter‑over syndrome provides further evidence of the cumulative psychological toll of extreme isolation. The Privacy Cloak Protocol is the structural countermeasure to the tendency toward sabotage documented in every confined industrial environment.

Metric for Success

Zero reports of colonists disabling or obstructing robotic sensors during deployment. Zero sensor failure incidents attributable to human interference.

