A.2 FAILURE MODE 2 — SENSOR POLLUTION AND THE SHARED LOCALIZATION GRID

Problem Statement

A lunar habitat module may contain a maintenance drone, a parked terrain vehicle running LiDAR, a medical assistant robot using infrared proximity, and structural monitoring pulsing RF signals—all simultaneously. Ultrasonic signals from one robot echo off metal walls and register as false obstacles for another. LiDAR pulses interfere with infrared detection. RF noise floors degrade every wireless system. The result is a navigation system making decisions based on partially fictional sensor data.

Architectural Solutions

Component 1: Acoustic and Electromagnetic Dampening Profiles

Parameter Specification
Application All interior surfaces in shared human‑robot spaces: walls, floors, ceilings
Material property Selected specifically to absorb—not reflect—ultrasonic, LiDAR, and RF signals
Specification timing Mandatory specification before any habitat component is manufactured. Cannot be retrofitted
Delivery Part of Cohabitation Engineer's earliest deliverable in design cycle

Component 2: Shared Localization Grid

Parameter Specification
Architecture Colony‑wide passive positioning infrastructure. Passive beacons embedded throughout habitat
Robot behavior Robots draw localization data from grid. No robot emits active scans in routine operation
Active scanning allowed Novel obstacle detection; close‑proximity collision avoidance in final approach; emergency navigation when grid fails
Status Colony utility infrastructure (like power distribution), not robotic system capability

Ultrasonic Interference Research

The problem of active sensor interference among multiple mobile robots is well established. Algorithms exist for synchronizing firing sequences of ultrasonic, IR, and laser sensors to eliminate interference, but these rely on communication between robots and do not address the fundamental signal‑pollution problem in environments where robots from different manufacturers operate independently. The Shared Localization Grid eliminates the need for active scanning in routine operation, bypassing the interference problem entirely.

Metric for Success

Sensor false‑positive rate from cross‑robot interference <0.1% during routine operations. Colonist self‑reported acoustic comfort scores above 90th percentile relative to Earth‑based analog habitats.

---

A.3 FAILURE MODE 3 — HUD FATIGUE AND THE EXCEPTION‑ONLY PERIPHERAL INTERFACE

Problem Statement

A lunar habitat with a dozen autonomous systems generates hundreds of trajectory recalculations per hour. If every recalculation that involves a human triggers a display notification, colonists will habituate to the system within their first week. Alert fatigue is documented in every safety‑critical environment—aviation, nuclear operations, surgical suites—and has led to disasters where critical alerts were ignored.

Research Basis: Alert Fatigue

Alert fatigue is well documented across safety‑critical domains. In healthcare, clinicians face hundreds of alerts from EPR systems daily, many low‑value or non‑actionable. Aviation solved this problem by ruthlessly prioritizing cockpit alerts so that only genuinely critical issues demand immediate attention. The same principle applies to human‑robot cohabitation.

Architectural Solution: Exception‑Only Peripheral Interface

Parameter Specification
Baseline state Invisible. Colonists receive no display, sound, or notification when robots are moving normally and trajectories are non‑conflicting
Activation trigger Robot's recalculated trajectory intersects with human's calculated path‑density footprint within 3‑minute window
Path‑density footprint Composite model: current location, declared task, historical movement patterns in this area of habitat, current velocity vector—the probabilistic volume of space the colonist is likely to occupy over the next 3 minutes
Alert modality Haptic (brief distinctive vibration pattern in worksuit or wrist device; vibration source indicates direction of robot approach) AND auditory‑spatial (directional low‑frequency hum spatially rendered through habitat audio system; hum appears to originate from robot's direction)
Visual display Available on demand only—never pushed to user
Interaction required None. Colonist's response can be entirely physical (step aside, pause, continue) without breaking cognitive engagement with primary task
Design principle Push exception, pull detail — the inversion that distinguishes a psychologically sustainable awareness system from one that trains users to ignore it

Metric for Success

Mean time to respond to collision alerts <0.5 seconds (haptic response without cognitive break). Zero documented cases of habituated alert dismissal leading to a safety incident during first six months of deployment.

---

A.4 FAILURE MODE 4 — KINEMATIC ILLEGIBILITY AND BIOLOGICAL DECELERATION CURVES

Problem Statement

Current autonomous navigation systems optimize for macro‑movement: getting from Point A to Point B efficiently and safely. They are not optimized for the micro‑movement legibility that human social cognition requires. A human takes a small step backward while facing away from a robot. The robot, reacting only to the changed proximity, halts abruptly. The human experiences this as startling. Repeated across a hundred interactions per day, it becomes a chronic low‑level stress signature.

Research Basis

A study on autonomous robot movement and human discomfort found that when robot motion was simple and predictable (straight‑line movement), participants habituated quickly and arousal levels decreased. When motion included unpredictable pauses and restarts, no habituation occurred—arousal and discomfort remained elevated throughout the experiment. A follow‑up study found that when the robot performed irregular stop‑and‑go behavior, participants reported significantly higher discomfort and showed increased skin conductance responses, with no habituation over repeated trials. The unpredictability disrupted not only the current interaction but also participants' expectations in subsequent trials.

Research Basis: Human‑Robot Proxemics

Construction site studies of human‑robot proxemics found that participants maintain a larger separation distance in crowded work conditions and when encountering a robot as a passerby compared to encountering a human. Quadruped robot research underscores the importance of adhering to human psychological and physical distancing norms in robot design.

Architectural Solution: Biological Deceleration Curves and Micro‑Yielding

Parameter Specification
Social awareness radius Distance from every detected human, substantially larger than collision‑avoidance radius. Within this radius, robot behavior changes qualitatively
Deceleration initiation At outer edge of social awareness radius—multiple meters before proximity would require any response—not at the safety boundary
Arc initiation Smooth early path adjustment away from human, beginning well before proximity would trigger collision avoidance
Spatial result Robot behaves the way a considerate person does—moving to create passing clearance early, at a trajectory angle that communicates intended path well in advance, at a speed that has already begun reducing before the human is close enough to require it

Micro‑Yielding Behavior Specification

Condition Robot Response
Robot stationary near human who shifts weight Small, smooth, immediate arc of chassis away from human's movement direction
Robot stationary near human who turns upper body Subtle reduction in any emitted operational noise
Robot stationary near human who makes any micro‑movement Minimal repositioning acknowledging human's kinetic intent—without waiting for human to physically displace
Design objective The robot should feel, to the human sharing space with it, the way a considerate colleague feels—present, aware, and easy to be near. Not invisible. Not subservient. Simply respectful of the social physics of shared space.

Metric for Success

Colonist physiological stress markers (ECG, EDA) during robot encounters indistinguishable from baseline human‑human encounter levels. Self‑reported comfort scores for robot encounters above 85% on validated scales.

