B.4 THE REGULATORY INFLECTION POINT: ISO 10218:2025

Earth has produced the most relevant safety framework the Cohabitation Engineer can learn from.

Major Revision: February 2025

Feature Change
Page increase ISO 10218‑1: 50 → 95 pages; ISO 10218‑2: 72 → 223 pages
First major revision since 2011
Integration ISO/TS 15066:2016 collaborative safety specifications integrated

The Critical Terminology Shift

“The terms collaborative robot and collaborative operation will not be found in the revised ISO 10218. Collaborative application is used instead, as only the actual use of the robot can be designed, tested, and confirmed as a collaborative application.”

Old term New term Implication
Collaborative robot (cobot) Collaborative application Safety is not a property of the robot — it is a property of the specific combination of robot, task, environment, and human interaction pattern

ANSI/A3 R15.06‑2025

The U.S. industrial robot safety standard, adapted from ISO 10218‑1/‑2:2025, likewise eschews the term collaborative robot, replacing it with the more precise concept of a collaborative application.

Application to Lunar Cohabitation

A robot that is mechanically safe but psychologically corrosive is a flawed collaborative application by definition. Every one of the five failure modes addressed in this blueprint is an application‑level failure, not a robot‑level failure. The Cohabitation Engineer's job is to design applications, not robots.

