What Differentiates the Cohabitation Engineer

None of the above programs explicitly address shared space cohabitation — the condition where humans and robots pursue parallel, sometimes conflicting objectives in the same physical volume for months or years without separation. The Cohabitation Engineer profession emerges from the intersection of three domains that are not currently taught together:

1. Robotics and autonomous systems (path planning, sensor physics, autonomy architectures)
2. Environmental psychology and isolation research (ICE effects, crowding, territorial behavior, human factors in confinement)
3. Industrial safety and human‑robot collaboration standards (ISO 10218:2025, collaborative application design)

Implication for Profession Definition

The Cohabitation Engineer is not a roboticist who has taken a psychology course. It is a distinct profession with its own body of knowledge, training architecture, and regulatory framework — one that must be established before the first lunar robot shares habitat space with the first lunar colonist.

